   "start": 0.0,
   "end": 28.36,
   "text": "The British scholar Jeffrey Haynes, who interviewed the key players over 30 years, puts it plainly. Rawlings wanted a revolution of popular democracy but never clearly defined the new system and lacked the ideological and institutional capacity to build it. He was brilliant at pulling an old order down and far less sure what to put in its place. Yet one piece did survive. In 1988, his government created 110 district assemblies, two-thirds of their members elected.",
